1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,397
Hello. In the previous episode,
I talked about why I started

2
00:00:05,437 --> 00:00:09,095
Articulate. The problem of trying to
understand an architecture when the

3
00:00:09,135 --> 00:00:12,280
knowledge is scattered across documents,
diagrams, repositories and in the

4
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:16,251
heads of different people. So
today, I want to answer the

5
00:00:16,291 --> 00:00:18,479
obvious question. What
exactly is Articulate?

6
00:00:18,519 --> 00:00:22,708
And the honest answer is,
it depends on when you ask

7
00:00:22,748 --> 00:00:27,592
me. So over the recent year,
I've described Articulate as a

8
00:00:27,632 --> 00:00:31,218
documentation tool, knowledge graph,
conversational assistant, an architecture

9
00:00:31,258 --> 00:00:33,879
modeling tool on a discovery platform.

10
00:00:33,919 --> 00:00:38,043
Every one of those descriptions captured
something I thought was important,

11
00:00:38,083 --> 00:00:42,194
but none of them captured
what I was really trying to

12
00:00:42,234 --> 00:00:44,080
build. Eventually, I realised something.

13
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:46,555
I wasn't trying to build a
better documentation tool.

14
00:00:46,595 --> 00:00:48,786
I wasn't trying to build
another modeling tool.

15
00:00:48,826 --> 00:00:52,151
I was trying to solve the
problem we talked about in

16
00:00:52,191 --> 00:00:55,498
the last episode, helping architects
understand complex systems.

17
00:00:55,538 --> 00:01:00,954
That changed how I started
to think about Articulate.

18
00:01:00,994 --> 00:01:06,389
Most architecture tools are built
around artefacts: documents, diagrams,

19
00:01:06,429 --> 00:01:09,064
decision records, source
code, infrastructure.

20
00:01:09,104 --> 00:01:11,224
All those things are valuable.

21
00:01:11,264 --> 00:01:13,384
I use them every day.

22
00:01:13,424 --> 00:01:16,883
But none of them are
actually architecture.

23
00:01:16,923 --> 00:01:20,188
They're just different views of it.

24
00:01:20,228 --> 00:01:25,200
The architecture is the understanding
that connects all of those things

25
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:29,495
together. Why decisions were made,
why assumptions were true at the

26
00:01:29,535 --> 00:01:33,400
time, and which systems depend on
each other, and what constraints

27
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:37,080
shape the design, and how
architecture has changed over time.

28
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:40,040
That's the thing I became interested in.

29
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:45,080
Not the documents themselves,
but the knowledge they represent.

30
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:50,064
One thing I've learned over the
years is that architecture never

31
00:01:50,104 --> 00:01:53,535
really stands still. Every
project teaches us something.

32
00:01:53,575 --> 00:01:57,115
Decision gets made. A
team discovers a problem.

33
00:01:57,155 --> 00:02:02,015
A service is replaced. A principle
emerges because something failed.

34
00:02:02,055 --> 00:02:07,000
Bit by bit, our understanding
of the architecture changes.

35
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:10,138
That's what architecture is really about.

36
00:02:10,178 --> 00:02:12,754
Not a collection of documents.

37
00:02:12,794 --> 00:02:16,415
A body of knowledge that's
constantly evolving.

38
00:02:16,455 --> 00:02:21,680
And if that's true, perhaps we've
been treating the wrong thing

39
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:25,640
as the primary asset. Maybe the
knowledge should come first, and

40
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:30,089
the documents and diagrams and
reports just become different views of

41
00:02:30,129 --> 00:02:34,793
that knowledge. That single idea
completely changed the direction of

42
00:02:34,833 --> 00:02:40,077
Articulate. Once I started thinking
about architecture as knowledge, instead

43
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:42,738
of documentation,
something became obvious.

44
00:02:42,778 --> 00:02:47,480
AI is remarkably good at
working with knowledge.

45
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:53,027
It can connect ideas, explain
relationships, and highlight

46
00:02:53,067 --> 00:02:57,511
inconsistencies. Challenge assumptions.
Find information that would have taken

47
00:02:57,551 --> 00:02:59,880
me hours to discover manually.

48
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:03,292
But that's not the role I want AI to play.

49
00:03:03,332 --> 00:03:07,224
I don't want AI making
architecture decisions for me.

50
00:03:07,264 --> 00:03:12,628
Architecture still requires
judgement, experience, trade-offs and

51
00:03:12,668 --> 00:03:16,366
accountability. Those responsibilities
stay with the architect.

52
00:03:16,406 --> 00:03:21,580
Instead, I see AI as a
collaborator asking me think more

53
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:26,200
clearly, helping me discover more
quickly, helping me see things I

54
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:32,773
might have otherwise missed. As
architects, we already work through

55
00:03:32,813 --> 00:03:37,635
conversation. We ask questions,
why was this designed this way?

56
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:39,298
What depends on this service?

57
00:03:39,338 --> 00:03:41,463
Have we solved this problem before?

58
00:03:41,503 --> 00:03:44,983
What happens if we change this component?

59
00:03:45,023 --> 00:03:49,929
Today, answering those questions usually
means searching through half a dozen

60
00:03:49,969 --> 00:03:53,400
different systems and hoping the
information is still accurate.

61
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:58,077
But if architecture becomes a connected
body of knowledge, conversation

62
00:03:58,117 --> 00:04:02,000
becomes the most natural
way to explore it.

63
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:07,040
Not because conversational AI is
fashionable, but because asking questions

64
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:09,640
is already how architects work.

65
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:15,380
So imagine joining a new organization
and instead of spending weeks

66
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:19,090
reading outdated documentation,
you simply ask questions.

67
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:23,709
Explain the payment system. Show
me everything that depends on this

68
00:04:23,749 --> 00:04:25,981
service. Why did we choose Kafka?

69
00:04:26,021 --> 00:04:28,925
What changed over the last 12 months?

70
00:04:28,965 --> 00:04:33,200
What systems could be affected
if we retired this application?

71
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:35,582
You're no longer searching
for architecture.

72
00:04:35,622 --> 00:04:37,963
You're having a conversation with it.

73
00:04:38,003 --> 00:04:40,758
And that's the future I want to explore.

74
00:04:40,798 --> 00:04:44,874
Not replacing architects, but giving
architects a better way to understand

75
00:04:44,914 --> 00:04:47,458
the systems they're responsible for.

76
00:04:47,498 --> 00:04:53,493
The phrase I've gradually started
using for this is Architectural

77
00:04:53,533 --> 00:04:59,360
Intelligence. Not Artificial
Intelligence. The ability to preserve

78
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:05,136
architectural knowledge, understand
relationships and reason about change,

79
00:05:05,176 --> 00:05:10,420
explain decisions and continuously
improve our understanding as new evidence

80
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:14,506
becomes available. I don't know whether
that vision is completely achievable.

81
00:05:14,546 --> 00:05:18,040
In fact, I expect many
of my ideas to change as

82
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:22,000
I build Articulate. That's
exactly why this project exists.

83
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:27,760
It's an exploration. A public engineering
journal documenting my understanding

84
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:31,760
as it evolves alongside the software.

85
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:37,568
So what is Articulate? Today,
I would describe it as an

86
00:05:37,608 --> 00:05:41,420
exploration into architectural
intelligence. An AI-native platform

87
00:05:41,460 --> 00:05:47,220
designed to help architects understand,
reason about and evolve complex

88
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:51,587
systems. In the next episode,
we'll build on that vision by

89
00:05:51,627 --> 00:05:55,587
asking another important question.
What does it actually mean to build

90
00:05:55,627 --> 00:05:56,680
an AI-native system?
